  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front brings showers and a few thunderstorms \nthrough late in the week.\n\nMid/upper jet forcing increases tonight through Thursday as a \nsurface cold front approaches from PA and WV. A line of thunderstorms\nover the OH Valley are expected to approach the Appalachian \ncrest in weakening form around or shortly after midnight. This \nactivity should continue to readily weaken as it runs out of \ninstability and forcing as it moves eastward toward daybreak, \nthough some showers or sprinkles can't be ruled out as far east\nas the Baltimore/Washington metro down through the Virginia \nfoothills and central Shenandoah Valley early Thursday morning.\nOtherwise a mild night is in store with southerly winds and\nelevated dew points in place.\n\nA strong but flattening mid/upper jet will swing by to the north\nThursday into Thursday night, with broad ascent in the right\nentrance region overhead. Increasing fgen and an associated\nfrontal wave look to pivot west to east across north-central VA\nlate Thursday afternoon into Thursday evening, then move east of\nthe Chesapeake Bay overnight. Limited heating through abundant\nclouds as well as poor lapse rates, high mid-level moisture and\nmodest moisture in the low levels due in part to downsloping\nflow will all keep instability limited. The greatest CAPE will\nlikely be around 1000-1500 J/kg across central VA where the most\nheating and highest low-level moisture content is anticipated.\n \nSome storms may tend to cluster heading into tomorrow evening \ngiven forcing along the front and increased flow aloft, but the \nmodest CAPE/DCAPE should keep any severe weather threat rather \nlimited for wind and hail. Still, can't rule out a briefly \nstronger, more organized rotating storm in the vicinity of the \nfront/wave in the evening across central VA to southern MD.\n\nThe front will slowly but steadily drop southward heading into\nFriday. Some shower activity may linger or redevelop in the\nvicinity of the front near the I-64 corridor, but most of the\ntime/area should be dry Friday unless the front slows down much\nmore significantly.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances continue \nthis weekend with hot temperatures return next week.\n\nOn Saturday, surface high pressure over New England shifts offshore \nas a cold front approaches from the northwest. Surface winds shift \nto southerly, ushering in a more humid airmass. With increased \nhumidity, showers and thunderstorms are possible Saturday and Sunday \nalthough are expected to remain below severe criteria. \n\nGoing into next week, precipitation chances linger but remain low \n(less than 30%). A low pressure system is currently forecast to \ntrack across the Great Lakes with the associated cold fronts \nimpacting the Mid-Atlantic. Temperatures will be in the upper 80s \n(upper 70s in the mountains) each day with high humidity values \nyielding heat indices in the low 90s for mountain valley regions or \nlocations east of the Blue Ridge.",
